### Why does the tests/ folder exist?

The tests folder holds the specs, and they exist as a separate layer so that what is being verified is expressed clearly and independently of how the UI is driven. Keeping specs thin means a reader sees the scenario at a glance.

```typescript
// tests/loan-approval.spec.ts
import { test, expect } from '../fixtures/bankFixtures';

test('Eligible customer loan is approved', async ({ loanPage, api }) => {
  await api.seedCustomer('CUST-001', { income: 60000, credit: 750, age: 25 });
  await loanPage.navigate();
  await loanPage.applyForLoan('CUST-001', 500000);
  await expect(loanPage.statusMessage).toContainText('Approved');
});
```

### Why does the pages/ folder exist?

Page Objects hold all locators and interaction methods for a screen in one predictable place. When the UI changes, you know exactly where to go, and specs never need editing for a locator change.

```typescript
// pages/LoanPage.ts
import { Page, Locator } from '@playwright/test';

export class LoanPage {
  readonly customerField: Locator;
  readonly amountField: Locator;
  readonly applyButton: Locator;
  readonly statusMessage: Locator;

  constructor(private page: Page) {
    this.customerField = page.getByLabel('Customer ID');
    this.amountField = page.getByLabel('Loan Amount');
    this.applyButton = page.getByRole('button', { name: 'Apply' });
    this.statusMessage = page.getByTestId('loan-status');
  }

  async navigate() { await this.page.goto('/loans/apply'); }

  async applyForLoan(customerId: string, amount: number) {
    await this.customerField.fill(customerId);
    await this.amountField.fill(amount.toString());
    await this.applyButton.click();
  }
}
```

### Why does the fixtures/ folder exist?

Fixtures hold dependency injection and lifecycle code so that setup and teardown are defined once and injected into tests automatically, rather than copy-pasted.

```typescript
// fixtures/bankFixtures.ts
import { test as base } from '@playwright/test';
import { LoanPage } from '../pages/LoanPage';
import { ApiHelper } from '../api/ApiHelper';

type Fixtures = {
  loanPage: LoanPage;
  api: ApiHelper;
};

export const test = base.extend<Fixtures>({
  loanPage: async ({ page }, use) => {
    await use(new LoanPage(page));
  },
  api: async ({ request }, use) => {
    const api = new ApiHelper(request);
    await use(api);
    await api.cleanup(); // teardown
  },
});
export { expect } from '@playwright/test';
```

### Why does the utils/ folder exist?

Utilities hold cross-cutting, UI-agnostic logic needed in many places. Without this folder, the same helper gets duplicated across pages and specs.

```typescript
// utils/moneyFormatter.ts
export function formatINR(amount: number): string {
  return new Intl.NumberFormat('en-IN', {
    style: 'currency',
    currency: 'INR',
  }).format(amount);
}

// utils/randomData.ts
export function randomAccountId(): string {
  return `ACC-${Date.now()}-${Math.random().toString(36).slice(2, 6)}`;
}

// utils/customAssertions.ts
import { expect, Locator } from '@playwright/test';

export async function expectAmount(locator: Locator, amount: number) {
  await expect(locator).toContainText(formatINR(amount));
}
```

### Why does the api/ folder exist?

API helpers create preconditions far faster and more reliably than clicking through the UI. They also verify UI actions at the source of truth.

```typescript
// api/ApiHelper.ts
import { APIRequestContext } from '@playwright/test';

export class ApiHelper {
  private createdIds: string[] = [];

  constructor(private request: APIRequestContext) {}

  async seedCustomer(id: string, data: Record<string, unknown>) {
    await this.request.post('/api/test/customers', {
      data: { id, ...data },
    });
    this.createdIds.push(id);
  }

  async cleanup() {
    for (const id of this.createdIds) {
      await this.request.delete(`/api/test/customers/${id}`);
    }
  }
}
```

### Why do data/ and config/ exist?

Data externalizes inputs so tests are data-driven and portable. Config externalizes environment settings so the suite runs anywhere by changing settings, not code.

```json
// data/loan-test-cases.json
[
  { "income": 60000, "credit": 750, "age": 25, "expected": "Approved" },
  { "income": 30000, "credit": 600, "age": 18, "expected": "Rejected" },
  { "income": 60000, "credit": 750, "age": 18, "expected": "Rejected" }
]
```

```bash
# Environment-specific runs
BASE_URL=https://staging.nationalbank.in npx playwright test
BASE_URL=https://qa.nationalbank.in npx playwright test
```

### Why does the reports/ folder exist?

Reports hold generated output and artifacts in a known location for the CI pipeline to publish and for engineers to diagnose failures. It is gitignored because it is generated.

```yaml
# CI publishes the report as an artifact
- name: Upload test report
  uses: actions/upload-artifact@v4
  if: always()
  with:
    name: playwright-report
    path: reports/
```

### What scaling properties does this structure give?

The structure embodies separation of concerns, which gives three scaling properties:

| Property | How the Structure Enables It |
|---|---|
| Changes are localized | UI change -> pages/, env change -> config/, data change -> data/ |
| Code is reused | Shared logic in utils/, api/, fixtures/ instead of copied |
| Structure is self-documenting | A new engineer knows where a spec, locator, helper, or config belongs |

```text
  Dependency flow (sensible direction):

  specs ──> pages ──> components
    │         │
    │         └──> utils
    │
    └──> fixtures ──> api
                      │
                      └──> utils

  Nothing depends on reports/
```

### What are common interview follow-up questions?

- "Do the exact folder names matter?" No. The principle is that each concern has a home and dependencies flow sensibly. Names are conventions.
- "How do you prevent pages/ from bloating?" Extract shared UI components into a components/ subfolder that pages compose.
- "How do you handle test data for parallel runs?" Generate unique data per test or per worker using factories in utils/ or API helpers in api/.
